<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sonora</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sonora is a state of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a>, with its capital at Hermosillo. A frontier province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, it was settled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missions and presidios among the Opata, Pima, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqui">Yaqui</a> and Seri, and was a base for expeditions to Alta <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>. In 1853–1854 the American filibuster <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-walker">William Walker</a> tried to seize it as a republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sonora.jpg" alt="Sonora" width="320" height="193" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tomascastelazo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:San_Carlos_Sonora_landscape.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Tuxtepec in 1876, and his regime strengthened the federal army and the rural police, the rurales, which were used against banditry and rebellion, notably in long campaigns against the Yaqui of <b>Sonora</b> and against the Maya of eastern Yucatán, whose stronghold of Chan Santa Cruz fell in 1901.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Soldaderas and camp followers, from young girls to middle-aged women and often of mestiza or indigenous origin, came from Oaxaca, Morelos, Tehuantepec, Central Mexico, <b>Sonora</b> and elsewhere.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-adelita">La Adelita</a></li><li><blockquote>…the English folk group The Spinners recorded it with their Caribbean singer Cliff Hall; the Cuban group La <b>Sonora</b> Matancera covered it in 1957 in English and Spanish, and the pianist Monty Alexander released a live version, recorded in 2012, on an album of 2014, calling it &quot;one…</blockquote><a href="/songs/linstead-market">Linstead Market</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sonora</b> Smart Dodd organised the celebration to honour her father, a widower who raised six children.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-19">19 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Teodoro de Croix became the first commandant general, with his seat at Arizpe in <b>Sonora</b>, later moved to Chihuahua.</blockquote><a href="/state/commandancy-general-of-the-internal-provinces">Commandancy General of the Internal Provinces</a></li><li><blockquote>In January 1854 Walker also proclaimed the Republic of <b>Sonora</b>, claiming the mainland state, and introduced the laws of Louisiana, including slavery.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-lower-california">Republic of Lower California</a></li></ul></section>
